00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.990
This is our next chapter.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.720
So why build our own
Linux distribution?

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.890
Four reasons.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.600
First, security.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.180
We own the end-to-end
supply chain.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.250
Every package that is built from
source and signed by Microsoft.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.520
You don't have to wonder where
your binaries came from.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.500
Second, performance.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.410
Azure Linux is a highly
performant image,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.410
optimized specifically
for Azure workloads

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.660
across a variety
of environments.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.220
Third, quality.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.170
We maintain a high-quality bar

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.260
to minimize outages
and disruptions.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.770
If tests fail, the
release does not ship.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.410
And lastly, support
and compliance.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.360
One vendor, one support channel,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.030
centralized support,
and compliance.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.840
This is a distribution
for Azure by Azure.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.490
And this isn't theoretical.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:05.100
Over 80% of Microsoft's Linux
usage runs on Azure Linux today:

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.590
Office, LinkedIn, Xbox,
Defender, Cloud Shell, AKS,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.460
App Services, and core
Azure infrastructure.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.180
Using Azure Linux allows these
teams to spend less time

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.570
