critical infrastructure.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.930
We trust it, and so can you.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.330
Now, let me walk you
through the architecture.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.030
Azure Linux is a
three-layer stack.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.450
At the foundation is
the Azure Linux Kernel,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.500
an upstream LTS Linux kernel
optimized for Hyper-V

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.270
across cloud and edge, with
Azure-specific silicon drivers

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:55.920
for better performance.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.840
It's hardened with
secure defaults

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.790
and supports multiple
architectures

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.390
like x86_64 and ARM64.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.590
On top of that is the Azure
Linux Core RPM packages,

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:11.190
but with a deliberately
small default footprint.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.520
You get what you need,
nothing that you don't.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.760
And wrapping everything is the
around-the-clock protection

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.500
from Microsoft.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.630
Every package is
built from scratch

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.030
to ensure supply chain security.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.910
Critical CVE patches
are available quickly

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.190
for commercially
supported images.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.610
We do monthly servicing
plus on-demand updates

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.350
for critical issues.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.530
Azure Linux runs
everywhere you need it.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.760
On AKS, it's been generally
available since 2023,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.850
running millions of cores in
