00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.500
Now, let me show you Azure
Linux 4.0 in action.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.570
I've already SSH'd into an Azure
Linux 4.0 VM running in Azure.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:28.050
First, let's confirm
what we're running.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.790
This is Azure Linux 4.0,

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.630
built on a Fedora foundation,
optimized for Azure.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:38.100
We're running the 6.18 kernel,
the latest upstream LTS kernel,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.310
hardened and tuned
for Azure workloads.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.820
One of the things that
makes Azure Linux 4.0 stand

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.390
out is SELinux is
enforcing by default.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.700
No extra configuration needed.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:54.390
You get mandatory access
control out of the box.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:58.500
And look at how lean this image
is: a small set of packages.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.890
A lightweight footprint means
fewer packages to patch,

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:06.000
fewer CVEs to deal with, and
a smaller attack surface.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.410
For package management, Azure
Linux 4.0 ships with DNF5.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.600
Fast, modern, and familiar

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.150
if you've worked
with Fedora or RHEL.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:19.170
Installing a package is
exactly what you'd expect.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.150
Finally, the firewall.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.150
Firewalld is active and
locked down by default.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.300
Only explicitly allowed
services are open.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:32.640
If I want to open a custom port
like 8080, I add it explicitly.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.700
Nothing is open
unless you say so.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:36.570
That's the kind

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:40.920
of secure-by-default posture we
want for production workloads.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.870
So that's Azure Linux 4.0.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:46.680
Secure by default with
SELinux and firewalld,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.380
lightweight with a
minimal package set,

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:53.510
and built on a modern
kernel optimized for Azure.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:56.000
Azure Linux 4.0 ships
with native support

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.820
for Azure services,
development tools,

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.580
and security features your
workloads already depend on.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:06.570
For security and observability,
you get Defender for Cloud

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.990
for robust security
management, and Azure Monitor

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:13.590
for comprehensive health
and performance insights.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.680
For custom image management,
Azure Image Builder

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.070
and Image Customizer let
you build, customize,

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.840
and deploy your own Azure Linux
images tailored to your needs.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.870
And for the developer
ecosystem, there's full support

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.470
for popular runtimes
like.NET and OpenJDK,

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.760
along with seamless
control through Azure CLI.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.300
Since general
availability in May 2023,

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:41.300
thousands of customers
have adopted Azure Linux

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.180
across financial
services, healthcare,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.150
video streaming,
and data analytics.

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:50.360
Customers running streaming
services have reported strong

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:54.680
reliability due to fewer
interruptions on Azure Linux.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.050
Large retailers have chosen it

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:00.510
for best-in-class
performance on Azure.

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:03.540
And customers in regulated
industries like healthcare

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:07.800
and finance who need strong
security guarantees have adopted

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.310
Azure Linux for its
reduced attack surface.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:15.810
The consistent feedback: Azure
Linux delivers on reliability,

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.630
performance, and security.

