00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.480
FLORA TAAGEN: Thanks, Poorvi.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.140
On the left, you can see a
typical AKS node running a

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.810
purpose-built version
of Azure Linux,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.750
including only the
essential components needed

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:45.810
to run Kubernetes
workloads efficiently.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:49.680
Azure Linux for AKS is a
lightweight operating system.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.490
Packages are deliberately
selected and rigorously tested

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.180
on AKS infrastructure, so
you get a minimal footprint

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.670
with high confidence
in stability.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.180
Azure Linux on AKS is available
wherever you need it,

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.870
in the cloud with AKS or at
the edge through AKS enabled

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.960
by Azure Arc, giving you
a consistent platform

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.910
across environments.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.860
And finally, security is built

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:17.130
into Azure Linux
on AKS by design.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:21.540
Azure Linux provides strong
compute-level isolation,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:25.500
and with options like an
immutable host configuration,

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:26.940
it helps reduce drift

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.700
and minimize attack
surface over time.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.720
Overall, Azure Linux with
AKS gives you a minimal,

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.480
well-tested, and
secure foundation

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.990
for running containerized
workloads at scale.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:41.610
At Build, we're excited

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.920
to announce the
general availability

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.190
of Azure Container Linux on AKS.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.890
Azure Container Linux, or ACL,

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:54.060
brings together Flatcar's
proven, vendor-neutral,

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.570
and immutable design with
the hardened security

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.020
and trusted supply
chain of Azure Linux.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:05.820
The result is a secure, scalable
container host, purpose-built

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.940
for running modern
workloads in production.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.850
ACL is based on
Flatcar's architecture

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.440
and delivers a minimal,

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.820
immutable operating system
optimized for containers.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.750
While it isn't binary compatible
with Flatcar, since it's built

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:25.200
on Azure Linux rather
than Gen2 sources,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.810
it provides the same
core functionality

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:32.190
and operational model that
customers rely on today.

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:36.120
Where ACL really stands
out is in its built-in,

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.000
out-of-the-box
security guarantees.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.140
First, immutability is
enforced at the kernel level.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.920
Critical system components like
the usr directory are locked

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:50.490
down to prevent drift and
unauthorized changes.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:55.140
Second, ACL uses a minimal,
purpose-built package set,

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.240
reducing the overall
attack surface

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.910
and lowering exposure to CVEs.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.000
Third, it includes strong
runtime protection

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.260
through SELinux, enforcing
strict least-privilege access

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.780
controls across the system.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.540
On top of that, ACL
supports Trusted Launch

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.810
with a unified kernel image,
ensuring the integrity

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:19.650
of the system from the
very first stage of boot.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.660
And finally, ACL benefits

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:25.770
from the same secure end-to-end
Microsoft supply chain

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:30.780
as Azure Linux, using signed
RPMs that you can trust.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.140
Together, this gives customers
a modern, immutable,

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:36.090
and security-first foundation

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:40.290
for running container
workloads on AKS at scale.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.810
This slide is here to
help you differentiate

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.570
between the two Azure
Linux-based container host

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:48.030
options on AKS.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.310
On the left is Azure Linux.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.320
This is the more
general-purpose,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.020
familiar Linux node experience.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.070
It uses the standard Kubernetes
host stack, and it's a great fit

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.920
for customers doing
lift-and-shift migrations

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:06.000
or who want a flexible, familiar
host environment while still

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.370
benefiting from Microsoft's
security posture

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.800
for AKS workloads.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.410
On the right is Azure
Container Linux.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:17.550
This is the more opinionated,
container-optimized option.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:21.030
It builds on Azure Linux
but adds stronger security

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.700
and integrity
guarantees by default.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:27.960
For example, a read-only
usr backed by dm-verity,

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:31.440
SELinux enabled, plus
an A/B update agent

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:33.480
and other hardening features.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.080
It's designed for cloud-native
customers who want a minimal,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.530
immutable host model similar
to what they may have seen

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:44.910
with other container-optimized
OSs in the ecosystem.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:46.560
Now the practical part.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.140
These options are not
mutually exclusive.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:53.370
In AKS, you can run an Azure
Linux node pool alongside an

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.580
Azure Container Linux node
pool in the same cluster

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.540
and schedule workloads to the
right host based on your needs.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:03.000
So the takeaway is,
choose Azure Linux

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.690
when you want the familiar,
flexible host experience.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:10.110
Choose Azure Container Linux
when you want the most hardened,

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.870
minimal, immutable
host by default.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.340
And if you have
mixed requirements,

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:17.250
you can use both
side-by-side in one cluster

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:21.000
by splitting workloads
across node pools.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.870
Now let's demo Azure
Container Linux in action.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:28.890
First thing we'll do is create
an AKS cluster running Azure

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.300
Container Linux.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:31.440
The only thing you need

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.860
to specify is os-sku
AzureContainerLinux

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.260
in the az aks create command.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:40.110
It's really that
simple to get started.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:43.140
Now, let me grab the credentials
so that I can interact

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:47.010
with the cluster, and let's
take a look at our nodes.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:50.040
You can see the node is
running Azure Container Linux,

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:53.100
and the OS image
column confirms it.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:56.640
Let's now take a look at
what's running on this node.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:58.830
First, the OS identity.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:01.590
You can see this is
Azure Container Linux,

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.260
built on the Azure
Linux package pipeline

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.080
with the same immutable
OS architecture

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.600
as Flatcar Container Linux.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:10.830
Here's the kernel.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:14.910
Azure Container Linux runs
the Azure Linux Kernel.

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:18.540
And bootctl shows us
the secure boot chain.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:23.040
You can see Secure Boot is
enabled with vTPM attestation,

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:27.930
and the OS uses a unified kernel
image, meaning the kernel,

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:32.820
ramfs, and the command line are
all signed as a single bundle.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:37.200
Now, let's look at how ACL
protects the system partition.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:40.200
Dm-verity is active on usr.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:44.550
This cryptographically verifies
every block read from disk.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.310
And if we try to write
to usr directly,

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:50.010
even as root, it's denied.

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:54.420
The file system is read-only
enforced at the block layer.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.650
ACL also ships with
SELinux in enforcing mode.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.920
So in summary, ACL gives
you defense-in-depth

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.330
at the node level.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:07.050
An immutable OS image
verified by dm-verity

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:10.440
and mandatory access
controls with SELinux.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:14.100
Even if an attacker gets root
access inside a container,

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:16.110
the host stays protected.

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:18.600
When it comes to Azure,
customers depend

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:22.860
on a diverse ecosystem of
Azure-native, third-party,

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:26.760
and open-source tooling
across DevOps, observability,

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.180
networking, and security.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:32.870
On AKS, Azure Linux enables
seamless integration

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.610
with the broader ecosystem,

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:37.220
supporting leading
partner solutions called

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.920
out on this slide, with
full compatibility

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:41.850
with Azure extensions.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.250
Azure Linux 4.0 for VMs

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:48.000
and Azure Container Linux
deliver strong day-one partner

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.840
support by supporting the
following subset of partners,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.690
accelerating readiness for
your production workloads

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.250
from the get-go.

00:17:56.250 --> 00:18:00.660
Azure Linux is deeply integrated
with the open-source ecosystem,

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:05.070
building on and contributing to
the upstream kernel and Fedora,

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.170
as well as key
community projects

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:11.460
such as Kata Containers,
containerd, and systemd.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:14.720
So why choose Azure
Linux as your OS?

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:18.140
Let's explore three core
benefits: resiliency,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.690
security, and performance.

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:25.100
Many customers managing
large-scale Azure workloads are

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:29.580
concerned about outages during
OS or application upgrades.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.740
In traditional distributions,

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:36.210
upgrades can introduce
frequent reboots, downtime,

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:39.230
or regressions that
disrupt services.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.570
Azure Linux mitigates this risk

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:44.360
through rigorous
end-to-end validation

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:48.150
across Azure scenarios
prior to release.

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.250
Any failure blocks the release,

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.190
helping prevent
customer-impacting issues

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.470
in production environments.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.510
Core components
such as the kernel

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:01.740
and critical system packages
are maintained on stable,

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:06.840
long-term support versions with
security patches backported.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:09.810
Changes are introduced
in a controlled manner

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:14.940
at release boundaries, avoiding
disruptive mid-cycle updates.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.510
Performance issues can impact
line-of-business apps,

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:22.770
and customers often blame
the OS for regressions.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:26.880
Azure Linux solves this with
a lightweight image optimized

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.580
for Azure, resulting
in faster pod

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.400
and node startup times on AKS.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:36.900
For example, MediaKind reported
faster node setup times,

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.480
and PlayFab saw quicker scaling

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:43.080
to handle player
influx on Azure Linux.

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:48.150
We regularly run performance
tests alongside quality checks.

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:52.230
If regressions appear, we
block the Azure Linux release.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:56.340
Data for cluster create and
cluster upgrade operations show

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:59.520
that Azure Linux is
consistently on par

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:02.280
or faster than other distros.

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:06.060
Security challenges
include CVE overload

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:07.920
and supply chain threats.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:09.750
Azure Linux addresses this

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:12.930
by reducing the overall
attack surface.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:15.300
Because we ship fewer packages,

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.810
there are simply fewer
CVEs to begin with.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:23.310
On top of that, we enforce
strict remediation SLAs,

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:27.810
with critical CVEs
addressed within five days.

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.870
At the kernel level,
all modules are signed

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.570
with Microsoft's trusted
key, which ensures

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.770
that only authorized code
can run in kernel space.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:41.040
And from a supply chain
perspective, everything is built

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.850
from source on Microsoft-managed
infrastructure,

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:48.060
giving you full control
and transparency.

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:52.710
Azure Linux also enables strong
out-of-the-box compliance

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:56.550
with FIPS images, CIS
benchmark alignment,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:01.080
and Secure Boot support
included at no additional cost.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.570
And here's the part I'm
most excited about:

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:07.590
how you can get involved and
started on Azure Linux today.

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:09.930
Everything is open source.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:14.310
Our GitHub is where you can
file issues and submit PRs.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:17.580
We actively review
community contributions,

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.230
and we want more of them.

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.690
You can get started with
our enterprise offerings

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:22.740
by following the links

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:25.740
to our documentation
included on this slide.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.710
And we run a bimonthly
community call.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:32.760
In this forum, we share roadmap
updates, demo new features,

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:34.710
and take feedback live.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.480
This is a community effort,

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:39.720
and your feedback directly
shapes what we build.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:43.770
So that's Azure Linux
end-to-end, modern, secure,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.950
and purpose-built for Azure.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:49.160
From Azure Linux 4.0 to
Azure Container Linux,

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:51.710
we're bringing a
unified foundation

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:55.200
across VMs, AKS, and containers.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.390
We're excited about
where this is going,

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:59.460
and we'd love your thoughts.

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:02.040
Questions, feedback,
please reach out.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:04.140
We look forward to
hearing from you.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.520
Thanks for joining us today.

